              "page": 3,
              "text": "Ideology in political science is a versatile concept that guides political behavior and policymaking. Scholars like Michael Freeden emphasize its role as the core of political language. Karl Marx's perspective highlights how ruling classes use ideology to maintain power, while Friedrich Hayek warns of the dangers of ideological planning, emphasizing individual freedom. These scholars show that ideology isn't just a set of beliefs but a powerful force shaping politics and society. Karl Mannheim, a pioneer in the study of ideology, defined it as \"a more or less systematic and integral set of beliefs and attitudes centering on the basic issues of metaphysics, epistemology, axiology, and praxeology.\""
